---
/**
 * Home `/` — three editorial sections, each a cover grid (W5 rebuild
 * 2026-09-06, replacing the board-panels + "최신 글" list):
 *
 *   Charts        · 올해의 앨범   — the nominate board. A LIST surface, so it
 *                                   shows and ranks scores (D2).
 *   Latest Reviews · 최신 리뷰     — a BROWSING surface: ArticleItem has no
 *                                   score field at all, so no figure can
 *                                   reach it (D2 enforced by the type).
 *   Notes         · 음악 이야기    — browsing, typographic cards (a story has
 *                                   no album art and never will).
 *
 * WHICH sections appear is decided in the derive layer (`homeSections`), not
 * here; the page renders the result and picks a layout from the count (P1).
 * Section order is also the score-exposure order: everything below the
 * Charts section is score-free, which is what build.dist-matrix asserts.
 *
 * ALL THREE SECTIONS ALWAYS RENDER (2026-09-07). R-4 ("빈 껍데기 금지") is
 * released FOR THESE THREE SECTIONS ONLY — see SectionEmpty for the scope and
 * for what R-4 still governs elsewhere. A section with no cards keeps its
 * heading and shows an empty, wordless area: no apology, and no sentence
 * describing what the section would contain.
 *
 * CARD SIZE NO LONGER DEPENDS ON CARD COUNT (2026-09-07, final form). The
 * browsing grids are ONE grid — four columns on the desktop, stepping to
 * 3/2/1 as the frame narrows — and the number of cards in a section changes
 * nothing about it. One card fills the first cell and leaves three empty;
 * four fill the row.
 *
 * This reverses two earlier passes, and the reversal is the point worth
 * recording. The grids were first `auto-fill` (the frame decided how many
 * columns), then count-driven (`n-1`…`n-4`, so three cards got three
 * columns) because an empty track on the right was read as a missing card.
 * The editor has since accepted the empty cells, and what that buys is the
 * property those variants cost: A CARD IS THE SAME SIZE ON EVERY PAGE LOAD.
 * Under the count-driven rule a section holding one review rendered it as a
 * 1231px feature and the same review as a 296px card the day a second one
 * was published — the reader's sense of "how big is a review card here" was
 * a function of how much had been published that week.
 *
 * The `n-*` variants and ArticleCard's whole `feature` layout were DELETED
 * rather than left dormant: a branch nobody can reach is a question the next
 * reader has to answer before they can change anything. Charts keeps its own
 * count classes — it is a carousel, a different object with a different job.
 *
 * The steps and the widths that trigger them are in the grid rules below;
 * the 320px track cap that keeps the hierarchy invariant true at every one
 * of them is unchanged, and deriveHomeSections still caps a section at four
 * — which is now exactly one full row rather than a coincidence.
 *
 * DENSITY IS THE HIERARCHY, AND IT IS A RULE, NOT A SETTING (2026-09-07).
 * Charts is what this site is for; the two browsing sections are not. Both
 * browsing sections take the SAME density on purpose — they are siblings, and
 * giving 음악 이야기 its own third size would invent a middle rank the
 * editorial structure does not have.
 *
 * The invariant, stated so it can be checked: A CHART CARD IS NEVER NARROWER
 * THAN A BROWSING CARD, at any combination of card counts.
 *
 * The first attempt at this failed and is worth recording, because the
 * failure is easy to repeat. "Charts keeps its grid, 최신 리뷰 goes 4-up" put
 * Charts at 5x195px against browsing's 4x248px and INVERTED the hierarchy it
 * was meant to create — the section that matters got the smaller card. Column
 * COUNT is not size. What fixes it is fixing the chart card's width so its
 * cell cannot shrink as the board fills, and capping every browsing track at
 * 320px so a thin section cannot balloon past a full one. Now that the
 * browsing card's width depends only on the viewport, the invariant has one
 * fewer variable in it — the measured table is in
 * 08-impl-notes/frontend.md.
 *
 * The two remaining variant faces are unchanged: `empty` (no reviews at all)
 * prints the two-line declaration, and `post-finalize` puts last year's
 * finalized-list card above the sections. `early` and `normal` now render
 * through the same path — the difference between them was the progress strip
 * ("올해의 앨범 후보가 여기서 자랍니다 — 지금까지 평론 n편"), which the brief
 * cut, and the card-count system covers the thin state better than a banner.
 *
 * The four variants stay distinguishable in the built HTML, which is what
 * the build e2e suite pins:
 *   empty         · the declaration line + three empty grounds, and not one
 *                   review link anywhere on the page
 *   early/normal  · a populated Charts grid carrying rank plates
 *   post-finalize · the finalized card + a Charts section whose empty ground
 *                   proves the NEW year's board starts empty (last year's
 *                   albums are gone from the home, not re-listed)
 *
 * Section heads carry a label and a title and nothing else (2026-09-07). The
 * Charts caption ("평론이 쌓일 때마다 바뀝니다 · 12월에 확정됩니다 · {날짜} 기준")
 * was cut with the same edit that cut /list/{year}/'s: a section that
 * explains what kind of section it is has stopped being a section and become
 * a note about the software. The heading names the surface; the cards under
 * it show what is on it.
 */
import ArticleCard from '../components/ArticleCard.astro';
import ChartCard from '../components/ChartCard.astro';
import FormatLabel from '../components/FormatLabel.astro';
import SectionEmpty from '../components/SectionEmpty.astro';
import SectionHead from '../components/SectionHead.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getSiteData } from '../lib/derive/site-data.ts';
import { SITE_TAGLINE } from '../lib/site.ts';
import { withBase } from '../lib/paths.ts';

const { site, homeSections, homeVariant, data } = getSiteData();
const { charts, latestReviews, notes } = homeSections;

// Charts: 1 / 2 / 3 are the three layouts, and everything above 3 is the same
// horizontal scroller, so the class saturates at n-3. The browsing grids used
// to have a matching helper and no longer do — see the intro.
const chartClass = (n: number) => `n-${Math.min(n, 3)}`;

// Exactly one <h1> per document. Now that all three sections always render,
// Charts is always the first heading on the page — except in the `empty`
// variant, where the declaration line above it is the page's real title and
// takes the h1 instead.
const chartsLevel = homeVariant === 'empty' ? 'h2' : 'h1';

const previousYear = site.active_year - 1;
const previousSnapshot = data.snapshots.find((s) => s.data.year === previousYear);
---

<Base title={site.site_name} description={SITE_TAGLINE} path="/">
  <main class="home">
    {
      homeVariant === 'empty' && (
        <section class="empty-state">
          {/* The same promise /about/ opens with, in the words /about/ now
              uses. This line used to read "…“안 들으면 손해” 문턱을 통과한
              것입니다", which was About's first statement verbatim; that
              statement was retired in the 2026-09-07 rewrite, and leaving it
              here would have stranded the site's front page on a sentence
              the site no longer says anywhere else. No new copy was written
              — this is the confirmed About sentence. */}
          <h1 class="lede">여기 실린 앨범은 전부 추천작입니다.</h1>
          <p class="status">첫 평론을 준비하고 있습니다.</p>
        </section>
      )
    }

    {
      homeVariant === 'post-finalize' && previousSnapshot && (
        <section class="finalized-card">
          <FormatLabel text={`${previousYear} 올해의 앨범`} />
          <a class="finalized-link" href={withBase(`/list/${previousYear}/`)}>
            <span class="finalized-title">{previousYear} 올해의 앨범 — 확정</span>
            <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </a>
        </section>
      )
    }

    <section class="board" aria-label="올해의 앨범">
      <div class="board-head">
        {/* `sheen` — the only heading on this page allowed the gradient
            display treatment: the annual list is what the scores produced,
            which is exactly what --accent is reserved for (tokens.css). The
            other two headings deliberately do NOT take it: "최신 리뷰" and
            "음악 이야기" are not lists the scores made, and painting them
            with the score's colour is the exact move that turns the doctrine
            back into decoration. */}
        <SectionHead label="Charts" title={`${site.active_year} 올해의 앨범`} level={chartsLevel} sheen />
        {
          charts.length > 2 && (
            /* Pager. These are ANCHORS, not buttons, and that is the whole
               progressive-enhancement story: with no script they are
               ordinary in-page links to the first and last card, which the
               browser scrolls the row to. The inline module upgrades them to
               step by one screenful and marks them aria-disabled at the ends.
               A <button> here would be a dead control without JavaScript. */
            <p class="pager">
              <a class="pager-btn" href="#chart-first" data-scroll="-1" aria-label="앞쪽 앨범 보기">
                <span aria-hidden="true">←</span>
              </a>
              <a class="pager-btn" href="#chart-last" data-scroll="1" aria-label="뒤쪽 앨범 보기">
                <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
              </a>
            </p>
          )
        }
      </div>
      {
        charts.length > 0 ? (
          /* <ol>, because markup order IS rank order within each bucket.
             Empty buckets contribute nothing here — the complete annual
             structure, empty buckets included, is one click away on
             /list/{year}/. */
          <ol class:list={['chart-cards', 'stagger', chartClass(charts.length)]}>
            {charts.map((entry, i) => (
              /* Only the first row is eager: three covers is the whole
                 above-the-fold image cost, the rest stream in on scroll. */
              <ChartCard
                entry={entry}
                feature={charts.length === 1}
                eager={i < 3}
                anchorId={i === 0 ? 'chart-first' : i === charts.length - 1 ? 'chart-last' : undefined}
              />
            ))}
          </ol>
        ) : (
          <SectionEmpty />
        )
      }
      {/* Charts keeps its tail link even with no cards: /list/{year}/ always
          exists and always renders every bucket, empty ones included. That
          page IS the annual structure this section summarises, so an empty
          Charts section can still point at something real — which is what
          "let the structure speak" means here. */}
      <a class="section-more" href={withBase(`/list/${site.active_year}/`)}>올해의 앨범 전체 보기 →</a>
    </section>

    <section aria-label="최신 리뷰">
      <SectionHead label="Latest Reviews" title="최신 리뷰" />
      {
        latestReviews.length > 0 ? (
          <>
            <ul class="cards">
              {latestReviews.map((item, i) => (
                /* showScore — D2-R2: this is one of the two surfaces the
                   editor reversed D2 for. 음악 이야기 below deliberately
                   does not pass it (a story has no score to print). */
                /* showFormat={false} — the section head one line above already
                   says "Latest Reviews", and nothing but a review can appear
                   here, so a REVIEWS chip on every card is the same word
                   twice. The mixed archives keep theirs (see ArticleCard). */
                <ArticleCard item={item} variant="card" compact eager={i < 4} showScore showFormat={false} />
              ))}
            </ul>
            {/* No tail link when the section is empty: the archive it points
                at would be empty too, and sending a reader to a second empty
                page is worse than not offering the trip. Charts is the
                exception above, and for a reason. */}
            <a class="section-more" href={withBase('/archive/reviews/')}>평론 전체 보기 →</a>
          </>
        ) : (
          <SectionEmpty />
        )
      }
    </section>

    <section aria-label="음악 이야기">
      <SectionHead label="Notes" title="음악 이야기" />
      {
        notes.length > 0 ? (
          <>
            <ul class="cards">
              {notes.map((item) => (
                <ArticleCard item={item} variant="card" compact showFormat={false} />
              ))}
            </ul>
            <a class="section-more" href={withBase('/archive/stories/')}>이야기 전체 보기 →</a>
          </>
        ) : (
          <SectionEmpty />
        )
      }
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .home {
    width: min(var(--shell), 100% - 2 * var(--gutter));
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: var(--s-48) 0 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--s-48);
  }
  .empty-state .lede {
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-size: var(--fs-27);
    line-height: 32px;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    font-weight: 800;
    margin: 0;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .empty-state .status {
    margin-top: var(--s-16);
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: var(--fs-14);
    line-height: 20px;
  }

  .finalized-card {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--s-12);
  }
  .finalized-link {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--s-12);
    min-height: 44px;
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-size: var(--fs-27);
    line-height: 32px;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    font-weight: 800;
  }
  .finalized-link:hover .finalized-title {
    color: var(--accent-2-ink);
  }
  .arrow {
    color: var(--accent-2-ink);
  }

  /* Section tail link — same object in all three sections. */
  /* Heading on the left, pager on the right, baseline-aligned. */
  .board-head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-end;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--s-16);
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
  .pager {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--s-8);
  }
  .pager-btn {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    min-width: 44px;
    min-height: 44px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
    color: var(--ink);
    font-size: var(--fs-16);
    line-height: 1;
    transition: background var(--t-micro), color var(--t-micro), border-color var(--t-micro);
  }
  .pager-btn:hover {
    background: var(--surface-2);
    color: var(--accent-2-ink);
  }
  /* Set by the inline module when the row is already at that end. Marked
     two ways — dimmed AND announced — never by colour alone (WCAG 1.4.1).
     The link still works; it simply has nowhere left to go. */
  .pager-btn[aria-disabled='true'] {
    color: var(--muted);
    border-color: var(--line);
  }

  .section-more {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 44px;
    margin-top: var(--s-16);
    font-size: var(--fs-15);
    line-height: 20px;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .section-more:hover {
    color: var(--accent-2-ink);
  }

  /* ── Grids: one rule set per card count (layouts.md §2.4) ──────────────
     Two grids, two densities. Chart cards are compact (cover + a 32px
     plate + two short lines), so five fit the 1040px frame; article cards
     carry a headline and an excerpt line and need 336px to stay readable. */
  .chart-cards,
  .cards {
    list-style: none;
    margin: var(--s-16) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
  }
  .chart-cards.n-2 {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s-16);
  }
  /* Charts, 3+ cards: a horizontal snap row, not a wrapping grid.
     Two problems solved at once. The board can hold fifteen albums, which as
     a 3-up grid was five rows of the home; and a wrapping grid divides the
     frame, so the more the board filled the SMALLER its cards got — the
     inversion this section is measured against. A scroller fixes the card at
     336px no matter how many there are, and the extra ones travel sideways
     instead of downward.
     `overflow-x` here is a CONTAINED scroll: the document itself never
     exceeds the viewport, which is what the 360px audit measures. */
  .chart-cards.n-3 {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--s-16);
    overflow-x: auto;
    /* Without this the row is free to grow to its content and the DOCUMENT
       scrolls sideways instead of the row — which the 360px audit correctly
       reports as a page-level overflow. A scroll container has to be allowed
       to be narrower than what it holds. */
    min-width: 0;
    scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
    /* The scroller starts and ends INSIDE the gutter, like every other
       block on the page. Letting a carousel bleed to the screen edge is a
       common magazine move, but this site's one explicit layout promise is
       that the frame never shifts between pages ("메뉴마다 가로폭이 튀는 게
       싫다") — and a bleeding row would break the left alignment line that
       the masthead, the headings and the cards below it all share. The
       cards that run past the edge are reached by scrolling, not by the
       container escaping.
       Room for the focus outline of a card at the edges, which a clipped
       scroll port would otherwise cut in half: */
    padding-bottom: var(--s-8);
    scrollbar-width: thin;
    overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
  }
  .chart-cards.n-3 > :global(*) {
    flex: 0 0 336px;
    scroll-snap-align: start;
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    /* One card per screen: below this width every grid on the page is a
       single column, and a peeking half-card would make the chart the only
       thing on the home that is not. */
    .chart-cards.n-3 > :global(*) {
      flex-basis: 100%;
    }
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .chart-cards.n-3 {
      scroll-behavior: auto;
    }
  }
  /* ── Browsing grids: ONE grid, four columns, stepped at three widths ──
     (2026-09-07. Replaced `auto-fill`, then the per-count `n-*` variants.)

        viewport      columns   browsing card       chart card
        ≥ 1160px         4      241 → 320px         336px
        840 – 1159       3      244 → 320px         336px
        641 – 839        2      292 → 320px         336px
        ≤ 640            1      full width          full width (tie)

     THE CARD COUNT IS NOT IN THAT TABLE, and that is the whole change: a
     section holding one card renders it in the first cell at the same width
     a section holding four would. The three empty cells are exactly that —
     empty. Nothing is drawn for a grid cell with no item in it, and every
     border, surface and radius on this page belongs to a CARD rather than to
     the grid, so a short section reads as a short row and never as a broken
     one.

     The breakpoints are where a track would otherwise fall under 240px, the
     narrowest a compact card stays readable at, computed against the gutter
     curve in tokens.css: 4 x 240 + 3 x 16 needs 1008px of content, which
     arrives at a 1160px viewport; 3-up needs 752 (840px); 2-up needs 496
     (641px, reused from the existing mobile breakpoint rather than invented
     at 540 so that every grid on this page collapses at the same width).

     `minmax(0, 320px)`, NOT `1fr`, IS THE HIERARCHY RULE IN CSS. A fixed max
     makes the track min(320, available/n): it fills the row when the row is
     narrow and STOPS at 320 when the row is wide, so a browsing card can
     never reach the chart card's fixed 336px at any width — including the
     ≥1680px case where the shell caps the frame at 1440 and four `1fr`
     tracks would resolve to 348px and invert the hierarchy. The `0` minimum
     is what keeps a card's own content (the nowrap subtitle) from blowing
     the track out at the narrow end. */
  .cards {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-16);
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 320px);
  }
  @media (min-width: 641px) {
    .cards {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 320px));
    }
  }
  @media (min-width: 840px) {
    .cards {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 320px));
    }
  }
  @media (min-width: 1160px) {
    .cards {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 320px));
    }
  }
  /* One chart card = the section's face; ChartCard owns the two-column
     feature layout. The browsing grids have no such case any more — see the
     intro. */
  .chart-cards.n-1 {
    display: block;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .home {
      padding-top: var(--s-32);
    }
    .chart-cards.n-2 {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }

  /* Board load orchestration — the site's ONE entrance animation:
     opacity + 8px rise, 240ms per card, 60ms stagger.
     Only the Charts grid animates: the motion vocabulary is two entries long
     and stays that way. Slots past the fifth SATURATE at the last delay
     rather than restarting from 0 — a card grid holds more items than the
     three-panel board did, and a restarted stagger reads as two separate
     animations rather than one entrance. */
  .stagger > :global(*) {
    animation: board-in var(--t-board-row) var(--t-board-ease) both;
    animation-delay: calc(var(--t-board-stagger) * 4);
  }
  .stagger > :global(*:nth-child(1)) { animation-delay: 0ms; }
  .stagger > :global(*:nth-child(2)) { animation-delay: var(--t-board-stagger); }
  .stagger > :global(*:nth-child(3)) { animation-delay: calc(var(--t-board-stagger) * 2); }
  .stagger > :global(*:nth-child(4)) { animation-delay: calc(var(--t-board-stagger) * 3); }
  @keyframes board-in {
    from {
      opacity: 0;
      transform: translateY(8px);
    }
    to {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: translateY(0);
    }
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .stagger > :global(*) {
      animation: none;
    }
  }
</style>
